       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · News18 India · 2026-10-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by News18 India, India Today">confirmed by 2 sources</span></div>
       <h1>President Murmu Appoints New Governors for Six States</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>President Droupadi Murmu has announced a reshuffle of gubernatorial appointments across six states. BJP parliamentary board member Dr. Sudha Yadav has been named Governor of Karnataka, while former BJP national general secretary Dushyant Gautam will head to Sikkim.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-12/president-murmu-appoints-new-governors-for-six-states.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Additional changes include Rajendra Vishwanath Arlekar moving from Kerala to Tamil Nadu, and current Sikkim Governor Om Prakash Mathur transitioning to Kerala. Furthermore, Braj Mohan Ram is appointed Governor of Odisha, and Dhal Singh Bisen will serve as Governor of Uttarakhand. The appointees will officially assume their duties upon taking charge of their respective offices.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures all six gubernatorial appointments precisely as detailed in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source · also covered by 1 more</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/india/six-states-get-new-governors-rajendra-arlekar-to-take-charge-in-tamil-nadu-dushyant-gautam-in-sikkim-ws-r-10378951.html" rel="noopener">News18 India</a></li><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/story/president-appoints-new-governors-in-6-states-rajendra-arlekar-becomes-tn-governor-op-mathur-gets-keralam-3015890-2026-10-12?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>This brief was written from the original report. The same event was independently covered by the other outlets listed here.</p>
    </div>
       
