     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Researchers Develop Ceramic Film to Improve Electronic Efficiency</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A multi-institutional team led by Penn State researchers has developed a ceramic thin film with unique magnetic properties that could lead to faster, more energy-efficient computing. The material, thinner than a human hair, utilizes a concept termed &quot;anomalous crystallinity&quot; to manipulate magnetic signals at the atomic level.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-12/researchers-develop-ceramic-film-to-improve-electronic-effic.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>By leveraging chemical disorder within the material's structure, the researchers created a method to drive magnetic signals that require less power than traditional electrical currents. Published in the journal Advanced Materials, the study suggests that integrating this technology could reduce device energy consumption and improve data storage capabilities, building upon a decade of research into high-entropy oxides.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report effectively synthesizes the research findings and correctly credits the Penn State-led team.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-chemical-disorder-ceramic-magnetic-properties.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
