     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Links Audit Office Staffing Structure to Improved Financial Accuracy</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>New research from the University at Buffalo School of Management indicates that higher levels of partner and manager supervision lead to higher-quality audits. Analyzing over 41,000 audits from 2008 to 2022, the study found that firms with larger proportions of senior supervisors experienced fewer financial misstatements and regulatory issues.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-12/study-links-audit-office-staffing-structure-to-improved-fina.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Effective oversight from managers and partners provides critical judgment, particularly during complex engagements. The relationship between staffing leverage and audit quality was notably stronger in offices with higher employee satisfaction regarding senior leadership. Researchers suggest that increased transparency regarding supervisory involvement could help investors better evaluate the quality of corporate audits.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is well-structured and accurately reflects the study's findings and methodology as described in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-staffing-office-outcomes.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
