       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · BBC Health · 2026-10-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Welsh Endometriosis Patients Face Surgery Cancellations and Funding Crisis</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#health</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Patients in Wales, including 32-year-old Kelly O'Shea, report undergoing operations only to find surgeons unavailable to complete complex endometriosis procedures. O'Shea, who paid 20,000 pounds privately for treatment, is among those affected by systemic resource shortages that have led to incomplete surgeries and service downgrades.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-12/welsh-endometriosis-patients-face-surgery-cancellations-and-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Gynaecologists and the Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists are calling for a nationally commissioned service or a funding model overhaul to address these challenges. The Welsh government deputy minister for women's health acknowledged the current system is unsustainable as hospitals struggle to meet accreditation requirements and prevent a potential exodus of clinical staff.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief captures the core issues regarding Welsh endometriosis services and the personal experience of the patient cited.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
